WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:14.800
Hello and welcome (back) to my channel

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:17.880
This time I will service and upgrade a coffee maker

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:21.080
The portafilter La Pavoni - Europiccola

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:24.240
A legend among espresso lovers

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:27.480
It has seen better times

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:30.560
And the chrome plating suffered the most

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:36.760
It was sold to me as defect

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:39.920
Lets see what might be the issue with it

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:44.000
It is my girlfirends birthday again

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:47.240
So I'll make sure it's a great gift

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.000
Good news the heating is still working

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:13.960
Apart from the leaking and the smut...

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:17.040
...everything seems okay

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:25.080
And it looks like some lime in the system

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:28.240
But water in electrics is a problem

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:35.480
Lets take it apart

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:30.160
Every little part can be removed...awesome

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:35.880
Was it ever serviced?

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:45.960
Behind this was also a gasket...

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:49.000
...which I only noticed off camera

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:54.720
Even the portafilter can be taken apart

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:22.080
All seals were hard and brittle

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:51.240
Drive out the pin

00:03:56.800 --> 00:03:59.800
Next I wanted to remove the level gauge

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:13.960
The glass pipe is protected by a metal casing

00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:24.920
Hot water with citric acid

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:28.040
But too much lime has settled

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:31.320
I cant push out the glass pipe

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:38.360
It has to wait

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:41.800
I continue with the electrics

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:45.160
2.79 - month and year of production

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:48.440
I take pictures of the wiring...

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:51.480
...before I remove everything

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:07.320
With hot air I soften the tin of the logo

00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:23.920
Next I remove the heating from the bottom

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:35.360
Mmhmmmm lime water

00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:39.200
This ring nut sits very tight

00:05:39.560 --> 00:05:42.560
I need to improvise with a self made tool

00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:47.080
Now i can grab it way better

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:07.720
Here again a broken gasket

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:27.080
Now again I want to remove the level indicator

00:06:27.760 --> 00:06:30.760
The nuts are very hard to reach

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:36.680
And filming this was even harder

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:40.200
I could only turn degree by degree

00:06:46.520 --> 00:06:49.520
But finally I could remove the gauge

00:06:57.400 --> 00:07:00.400
And the glass was unharmed

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:13.960
So I had to leave it on the kettle

00:07:15.640 --> 00:07:18.640
Now I can start building it up again

00:07:19.160 --> 00:07:22.160
The base has some rust on it

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:27.240
Thats the only part I sandblast

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:30.520
The other parts can be refurbished

00:07:31.880 --> 00:07:34.880
I use corundum as blasting medium

00:07:34.920 --> 00:07:37.920
Medium grain 0.3-0.5mm

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:01.320
Afterwards I clean it with acetone

00:08:06.320 --> 00:08:09.320
I continue with a primer powder

00:08:19.120 --> 00:08:22.120
Baking for 15 minutes at 180°C

00:08:25.560 --> 00:08:28.560
Before I make the final coat...

00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:31.960
...I sand it with 800 grit 

00:08:32.360 --> 00:08:35.360
This removes last imperfections

00:08:47.120 --> 00:08:50.120
I choose RAL 6027 “light green”

00:08:50.520 --> 00:08:53.520
Its a nice color to represent the 70s

00:09:03.080 --> 00:09:06.080
Again 15 minutes at 180°C

00:09:17.880 --> 00:09:20.880
Of course I made sure that this is saved

00:09:33.400 --> 00:09:36.400
Next I need to get rid of all the lime

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:39.960
With citric acid and the ultrasonic cleaner...

00:09:40.040 --> 00:09:43.040
...It shouldnt be a problem

00:09:43.080 --> 00:09:46.080
100g on 5 Liter

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:49.320
Bathing 15 minutes at 95°C

00:09:56.360 --> 00:09:59.360
Looking great

00:10:01.960 --> 00:10:04.960
No lime left on the parts

00:10:05.960 --> 00:10:08.960
I did this with all parts

00:10:26.480 --> 00:10:29.480
Next I need to see if the chrome can be saved

00:10:32.680 --> 00:10:35.680
I only polish with finest compound

00:10:35.880 --> 00:10:38.880
And also only on the soft buffing wheel

00:10:49.840 --> 00:10:52.840
This takes longer, but is the safest way

00:10:58.800 --> 00:11:01.800
Its better than I expected

00:11:04.440 --> 00:11:07.440
I also polish all other chrome parts

00:11:18.920 --> 00:11:21.920
The chrome layer on the steam nozzle has worn off

00:11:22.680 --> 00:11:25.680
With tampon galvanic I will restore it

00:11:25.920 --> 00:11:28.920
Hose down with distilled water

00:11:29.880 --> 00:11:32.880
And heat the chrome electrolyte to 60°C

00:11:37.600 --> 00:11:40.600
Fast movements are important

00:11:40.840 --> 00:11:43.840
Otherwise it turns black

00:11:44.200 --> 00:11:47.200
I only use around 0.8 Amps

00:11:53.080 --> 00:11:56.080
Cleaning again with destilled water

00:11:56.440 --> 00:11:59.440
And back is the chrome

00:12:10.800 --> 00:12:13.800
Now I need to polish the brass a bit

00:12:14.000 --> 00:12:17.000
I will only use steel wool for this

00:12:17.040 --> 00:12:20.040
Degree of fineness of the steel wool is #000

00:12:55.960 --> 00:12:58.960
Now the thing is...I hate plastic

00:12:59.320 --> 00:13:02.320
Lets use birdseye maple instead

00:13:02.760 --> 00:13:05.760
I will recreate all those parts

00:13:05.960 --> 00:13:08.960
Use the wood efficiently

00:13:21.080 --> 00:13:24.080
Next I have to put threads into the wood

00:13:24.200 --> 00:13:27.200
I drill holes for that

00:13:32.440 --> 00:13:35.440
Then I wanted to glue in the thread with PU glue

00:13:35.600 --> 00:13:38.600
But the holes were 0.03mm too wide

00:13:41.840 --> 00:13:44.840
So I had to roughen the surface of the thread

00:13:45.120 --> 00:13:48.120
No problem thanks to rough treatment...

00:13:48.320 --> 00:13:51.320
...with the knurling tool

00:14:00.400 --> 00:14:03.400
I now press in the thread with the lathe 

00:14:04.160 --> 00:14:07.160
I use PU glue since it works good for metal and wood

00:14:21.840 --> 00:14:24.840
I start with the Roughing-Out-Gouge

00:14:27.840 --> 00:14:30.840
Afterwards I use the chisel to refine the surface 

00:14:43.920 --> 00:14:46.920
I sand everything from 120-800 grit

00:14:54.440 --> 00:14:57.440
I love the birdseye grain

00:15:04.320 --> 00:15:07.320
Next the tricky parts

00:15:07.640 --> 00:15:10.640
I had to think long about this process

00:15:10.760 --> 00:15:13.760
Replacing star screws is not easy

00:15:16.320 --> 00:15:19.320
First I need a cylinder again

00:15:37.640 --> 00:15:40.640
I then built a drilling aid with wood scraps

00:15:44.200 --> 00:15:47.200
The drilling aid holds the cylinder straight and in place...

00:15:47.400 --> 00:15:50.400
...and helps me to align it

00:15:51.360 --> 00:15:54.360
I wrapped it in tape to avoid breakouts btw

00:16:06.640 --> 00:16:09.640
Okay that worked great

00:16:10.240 --> 00:16:13.240
Now I can continue turning the final form

00:16:24.840 --> 00:16:27.840
I do this freestyle 

00:16:52.520 --> 00:16:55.520
At the end I chisel off a straight edge at the bottom

00:16:57.240 --> 00:17:00.240
We are getting there

00:17:11.400 --> 00:17:14.400
I was so relieved when I was done

00:17:17.680 --> 00:17:20.680
I seal everything with hard wax oil

00:17:24.640 --> 00:17:27.640
I do 3 coats with 1 day drying time in between

00:17:41.240 --> 00:17:44.240
Between each coat I sand with 1000 grit paper

00:17:58.760 --> 00:18:01.760
But I still need a thread for the top screw

00:18:03.520 --> 00:18:06.520
I take the thread of the old locking screw

00:18:33.800 --> 00:18:36.800
Both parts will be connected with a custom screw

00:18:37.960 --> 00:18:40.960
Now i make the custom screw

00:19:12.360 --> 00:19:15.360
Now I clean the glass with citric acid

00:19:45.840 --> 00:19:48.840
I clean the rubber base with pure alcohol

00:20:06.200 --> 00:20:09.200
I am replacing the cable mount

00:20:10.000 --> 00:20:13.000
And clean logo and switch with alcohol

00:20:20.200 --> 00:20:23.200
Finally, I replace all the cables

00:20:55.360 --> 00:20:58.360
Everything is ready for assembly

00:21:00.080 --> 00:21:03.080
But before that it's time for a beer

00:21:04.120 --> 00:21:07.120
This time I test a “Schönbuchbräu”

00:21:07.400 --> 00:21:10.400
A very local beer from Stuttgart

00:21:10.760 --> 00:21:13.760
I will rate Entry, Aftertaste, Body...

00:21:13.880 --> 00:21:16.880
...Colour, Bitterness, Acidity and Fizz...

00:21:17.040 --> 00:21:20.040
...on a scale of 1 = bad till 6 = great.

00:21:20.200 --> 00:21:23.200
I rate one beer in each video

00:21:29.120 --> 00:21:32.280
The colour is a strong and rich golden yellow

00:21:32.840 --> 00:21:35.600
But it seems there is no fizz

00:21:36.080 --> 00:21:38.840
The foam vanishes instantly

00:21:39.320 --> 00:21:41.840
Lets see how it tastes

00:21:42.040 --> 00:21:45.040
And here are the results

00:22:08.160 --> 00:22:10.920
Let's get back to work

00:22:11.240 --> 00:22:14.240
It goes without saying that I renew every seal

00:22:14.400 --> 00:22:17.400
And I use food-grade loctite

00:22:42.840 --> 00:22:45.840
Sorry for this footage :D

00:23:15.160 --> 00:23:18.160
To tighten the ring nut again...

00:23:18.480 --> 00:23:21.480
...my friend Sven printed me an assembly aid

00:23:38.160 --> 00:23:41.160
Thank you Sven

00:26:06.520 --> 00:26:09.520
I use silicone grease from the manufacturer

00:26:50.800 --> 00:26:53.800
The rubber will be pressed in later with the portafilter

00:30:25.960 --> 00:30:28.960
Aaaaaaaaand its done

00:30:32.120 --> 00:30:33.800
Before

00:30:34.080 --> 00:30:37.080
After

00:30:52.520 --> 00:30:55.520
If you like it please leave a comment and SHARE

00:31:29.320 --> 00:31:32.320
Lets see if I am able to use it

00:31:47.680 --> 00:31:50.680
Please leave me tips for a good coffee in the comments

00:31:50.680 --> 00:31:53.680
I’m new to those coffee crafts

00:31:55.160 --> 00:31:58.160
I use my restored coffee grinder of course

00:32:13.040 --> 00:32:16.040
I have read that it must be 14g of coffee

00:32:46.360 --> 00:32:49.360
I’m proud of this first attempt

00:32:50.800 --> 00:32:53.800
But I’m eager to learn how to improve my coffee game

